# Effect of education expenditure on per-capita output: SSA vs developing (LMC) countries

## Data
WDI-based group aggregates, 1975-2015 (41 yrs). Groups: Sub-Saharan Africa (SSF) vs Lower middle income (LMC).
Variables: education expenditure % of GNI (NY.ADJ.AEDU.GN.ZS), GNI per capita 2015 US$ (NY.GNP.PCAP.KD),
exports annual % growth (NE.EXP.GNFS.KD.ZG), primary/secondary gross enrollment, labor-force participation.
KEY DATA LIMIT: GNI per capita exists for SSA only in 2011-2015 (5 yrs); it is complete for LMC.
Exports growth: SSA 1991-2015, LMC 1975-2015. Education expenditure & enrollments: complete both groups.

## Method
ARDL(1,1): y_t = a + rho*y_{t-1} + beta*edu_t + delta*edu_{t-1} + controls + e, Newey-West HAC errors.
Endogeneity/autoregression handled via lagged dependent variable, lagged education expenditure,
and a lag-only (no contemporaneous) variant. Long-run multiplier LR=(beta+delta)/(1-rho), delta-method CI.
Group comparison via stacked interaction model + nonlinear Wald test of LR equality.
Primary comparable outcome = exports growth (economic-output proxy), common 1991-2015 window.
Direct outcome = GNI per-capita growth (LMC regression; SSA descriptive only).

## Primary: exports growth, 1991-2015 (the statistically comparable comparison)
| Group | LR | se | 95% CI | persistence rho |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| SSA (SSF) | +4.68 | 2.17 | [0.43, 8.94] | 0.11 |
| LMC (developing) | +5.96 | 3.72 | [-1.33, 13.24] | 0.12 |
Difference (LMC - SSA) = +1.27 (se 4.37); Wald z = 0.29, p = 0.771 -> NOT significant.

## Robustness (exports outcome)
- Lag-only (edu_l1, edu_l2): SSA LR +6.76 (CI [2.12, 11.41], sig); LMC +1.70 (CI ns); diff -5.07 (se 6.05), p=0.402 -> still no significant group difference.
- + Labor-force control: SSA -1.71 (ns), LMC +1.89 (ns) -> SSA sign flips, but spec is unstable (LF near-constant).
- Enrollments (primary/secondary) as intermediate outcome: near-unit-root persistence (rho 0.96-1.01) makes LR non-identifiable (huge CIs); short-run coefficients mostly insignificant --> no robust human-capital accumulation effect.

## Direct evidence: GNI per-capita growth
- LMC full 1975-2015: LR -3.08 (se 3.66, CI [-10.25, 4.09], NOT significant).
- LMC matched 1991-2015 (+exports control): LR -2.30 (se 1.05, CI [-4.36, -0.23], significant negative; fragile spec).
- SSA: descriptive ONLY - 4 growth observations (2012-2015), mean +1.24%/yr, corr(edu, GNI growth) = -0.56. NO cross-group test possible on this variable.

## Proxy validity
corr(exports growth, GNI per-capita growth): LMC full r=0.74; LMC 1991-2015 r=0.48; SSA r=0.94 (n=4).

## Final data-grounded hypothesis
The effect of increasing education expenditure (% of GNI) on per-capita economic output is statistically
indistinguishable between Sub-Saharan Africa and developing (lower-middle-income) countries. Using exports
growth as the economic-output proxy (the only outcome statistically comparable across both groups), the
estimated long-run effect of a one-percentage-point rise in education expenditure is +4.7 pp for SSA
(95% CI 0.4 to 8.9) and +6.0 pp for developing countries (95% CI -1.3 to 13.2), a between-group difference
of only +1.3 pp that is clearly insignificant (Wald p = 0.77). Point estimates are positive in both groups
but imprecise and economically implausible in magnitude, and the direct GNI per-capita-growth evidence is
inconsistent (LMC point estimates negative; SSA un-estimable, n=4), so no robustly positive or materially
different effect of education spending on per-capita output can be established between the two groups.
